We already studied the Tychonoff's theorem using a filter. In here, we are
going to prove the same theorem but in net.

useenv thm [Tychonoff's Theorem] {
    Let $\{X_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}$ be a collection of compact topological
    spaces where $A$ is an index set. Then the space
    $X=\prod_{\alpha\in A}X_\alpha$ equipped with the product topology is
    compact.
    \proof
    Let us define a projection $\pi_\alpha:X->X_\alpha$ for each $\alpha\in A$,
    and fix a universal net $(x_\lambda)_{\lambda\in\Lambda}$ in $X$. By
    \Cref{prop:pushforward-universal}, $(\pi_\alpha)_*(x_\lambda)$ is a
    universal net in $X_\alpha$, so we can take a point $z_\alpha\in X_\alpha$
    such that $(\pi_\alpha)_*(x_\lambda) -> z_\alpha$. Such points exists
    because $X_\alpha$ is compact and recall \Cref{thm:compact-net}. Finally,
    take the point $x\in X$ such that $\pi_\alpha(x)=z_\alpha$ for every
    $\alpha\in A$. Claim that $x_\lambda -> x$.

    To prove this, fix an open neighborhood $N$ of $x$ in $X$. Since
    $$
        \{\pi_\alpha^{-1}[U] : \alpha\in A, U\text{ open in } X_\alpha\}
    $$
    forms a subbasis of $X$, there exists $\alpha_1,...,\alpha_n\in A$, and
    $U_{\alpha_1},...,U_{\alpha_n}$ are open sets in $X_{\alpha_j}$,
    $j=1,2,...,n$, respectively, such that
    $$
        x\in \pi_{\alpha_1}^{-1}[U_{\alpha_1}]\cap\pi_{\alpha_2}^{-1}[U_{\alpha_2}]
        \cap...\cap\pi_{\alpha_n}^{-1}[U_{\alpha_n}]\subset N.
    $$
    Notice that
    $$
        z_{\alpha_j}\in U_{\alpha_j}
    $$
    for $j=1,2,...,n$. This gives that $U_{\alpha_j}$ eventually contains
    $(\pi_{\alpha_j})_*(x_\lambda)$, hence $\pi_{\alpha_j}^{-1}[U_{\alpha_j}]$
    eventually contains $(x_\lambda)_{\lambda\in\Lambda}$.

    Thus,
    useenv align* {
        (x_\lambda)_{\lambda\in\Lambda} \text{ is eventually in } &
        \pi_{\alpha_1}^{-1}[U_{\alpha_1}]\cap\pi_{\alpha_2}^{-1}[U_{\alpha_2}]
        \cap...\cap\pi_{\alpha_n}^{-1}[U_{\alpha_n}] \\
        &=> N\text{ eventually contains }(x_\lambda)_{\lambda\in\Lambda}.
    }

    Since $N$ is arbitrary open neighborhood of $x$, we have proved that
    $x_\lambda -> x$. Therefore, $X$ is compact because of
    \Cref{thm:compact-net}.
    \proved
}
